      "text": "Introduction",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Vitamin D and PTH play an important role in calcium homeostasis",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "skin, liver, parathyroid gland, kidney, bone, and small intestine all play a role",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "increased PTH and Vitamin D leads to increase serum calcium levels",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Vitamin D Physiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Overview",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/9091/images/vitamin%20d%20and%20pth%20metabolism.jpg",
              "caption": "Combined"
            },
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/9091/images/jci0629449_f3_moved.jpg",
              "caption": ""
            }
          ],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Vitamin D and PTH play an important role in calcium homeostasis",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "skin, liver, parathyroid gland, kidney, bone, and small intestine all play a role",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "Increased PTH and Vitamin D leads to increase serum calcium levels",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Synthesis",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**7-Dehydrocholesterol**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "precursor to calcitriol is stored in the skin where UV exposure converts it to previtamin D3.",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**cholecalciferol (Vitamin D3)**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "Previtamin D3 is then bound to **vitamin-D binding protein** (DBP) where it is carried to the liver and metabolized to **25-hydroxyvitamin D3**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**25-hydroxyvitamin D3**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "when calcium is low, **parathyroid hormone (PTH)** levels become elevated which activates 1-alpha-hydroxylase in the kidney",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**1-alpha-hydroxylase** converts 25-hydroxyvitamin D to the active Vitamin D (calcitriol)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "laboratory study of choice to determine Vitamin D deficiency",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D3 (Vitamin D, calcitriol)**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "active form that controls calcium homeostasis in body by targeting intestines and bones (see function below)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Function",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**↑ serum Ca**2+ **and phosphate via**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "↑ absorption of calcium and phosphate from the intestine",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "↑ bone resorption of Ca2+ and phosphate",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "recall **PTH** functions to ↑ serum Ca2+ but **↓ serum phosphate**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Regulation",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "PTH stimulates 1,25-(OH)2 vitamin D production",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "hypocalcemia/hypophoshatemia stimulates 1,25-(OH)2 vitamin D production",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "1,25-(OH)2 vitamin D feedback negatively on itself",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Bone Healing",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Serves as an important regulator of the inflammatory response at the site of injury",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Promotes vasculogenesis and invasion into the wound healing bed by regulating VEGF and other growth factors",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Stimulates mesenchymal stem cell (MSC) commitment to osteoblastic lineage through adhesion to matrix proteins and the expression of integrins",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Stimulates the production of collagenous and non-collagenous extracellular matrix (ECM) proteins including collagen I, osteocalcin, and osteopontin",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Potential for an increased rate of fracture and arthrodesis nonunion with vitamin D deficiency",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "PTH Physiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Synthesis",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "secreted by the **chief cells of parathyroid**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Function",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**↑ serum Ca2+ and ↓ serum phosphate in response to hypocalcemia/hypomagnesemia** via",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "↑ bone resorption of calcium and phosphate (bone is destroyed)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "PTH receptor is on the osteoblasts which secretes IL-1 to activated osteoclasts",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "↑ kidney resorption of calcium in distal convoluted tubule",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "↓ kidney resorption of phosphate",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "↑ 1,25-(OH)2 vitamin D production",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
